# Building OpenAstroExplorer: Learning to Understand the System

I have always found astrophotography fascinating because of the contrast between the simplicity of the final image and the complexity required to produce it. A photograph of a galaxy or nebula may appear almost effortless once processed, yet behind it sits a system of optics, mechanical tracking, electronics, software and mathematics, all of which have to work together with a surprising degree of precision.

That complexity is what drew me towards building an OpenAstroExplorer.

OpenAstroExplorer is an open-source GoTo equatorial mount: a motorised system designed to compensate for the Earth's rotation, track astronomical objects and automatically slew a camera towards coordinates in the night sky. I initially approached the project as a way of improving my astrophotography setup whilst making use of components I already had from an old 3D printer. In reality, it became much broader than that. Building it forced me into areas of electronics, mechanical design and software that I had little experience with, and exposed the difference between assembling individual components and actually understanding the system they form.

The project was often frustrating. Motors refused to move, drivers became unexpectedly hot, mechanical components developed backlash, computers refused to boot and software occasionally appeared to have a completely different understanding of where the telescope was pointing than I did.

Very little worked perfectly the first time, and most of the useful learning came from understanding why.

